# Scale & list UX (thousands of records)

**Always assume lists can grow to thousands of rows.** Do not wait for the user to remind you. Apply this on every list, table, ledger, activity feed, or nested section (orders under a customer, wallet under a customer, claims, admin consoles, buyer history, etc.).

## Required by default

- **Server-side pagination** (or cursor / offset load-more that fetches the next page from the API). Never load “all” into the browser.
- **Page size** ~20–40. Prev/Next or “More” that appends the **next API page** only.
- **Search / filter** for the primary key people use (order #, phone, name, status, date range).
- **Do not** dump hundreds of rows on one screen. **Do not** use client-only “Show all” that reveals a huge in-memory list as the long-term design.
- Nested panels (e.g. Customer → Wallet / Orders) follow the same rules as top-level pages.

## Density

- Short labels; one job per section.
- Summary first; expand detail on demand.
- Prefer tabs or separate sections over stacking two unbounded lists.

## Backend

- Prefer DB/API `page`/`size` or `limit`/`offset` + `hasMore` / `totalPages`.
- If only a capped first page exists today, wire UI to that and load more via API — do not pretend the first 20 is the full history without a More control that pages.

## Process

If you ship a new list without pagination/search, that is incomplete. Fix scale in the same change unless there is a hard reason (fixed ≤10 item checklist).
